---
description: Forbid any use of git reset and require safe, non-destructive recovery steps.
alwaysApply: true
---

### Do not use git reset

- Under no circumstances run `git reset` in any form.
- This includes `git reset --hard`, `git reset --mixed`, `git reset --soft`, any revision like `git reset HEAD~1`, and path-based resets.

### Rationale

- Multiple editors and terminals may be open at once.
- Code may have uncommitted edits across tabs and panes.

### Required approach

- Never rewrite or wipe work. Use non-destructive steps only.
- First take a safety snapshot:
  - `git switch -c safety-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)` or `git checkout -b safety-...`
  - Then either `git add -A && git commit -m "safety snapshot"` or `git stash push -u -m "safety snapshot"`
- Investigate and plan using read-only commands:
  - `git status`, `git diff`, `git log --oneline --graph --decorate`
- Restore only what you mean to change, by path:
  - `git restore --worktree path1 path2`
  - `git restore --staged path1` to unstage without discarding content
- To back out a bad commit without losing work, prefer:
  - `git revert <commit>`
  - or create a new branch and cherry-pick the good commits
- If a tool believes packages or setup are needed, only display the commands to the user. Do not execute them.

### Prohibited fallbacks

- Do not use `git clean -fdx` as a substitute.
- Do not use bulk wipes like `git checkout .` or `git restore --source=HEAD --worktree :/`.
